Source: tmd
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Bryan C. Everly <bryan@theeverlys.com>
# Deliberately short, and it is meant to stay that way. tmd is C with no
# dependencies beyond libc: there is nothing to vendor, nothing to fetch, and a
# Launchpad builder -- which has no network -- can build it from the source
# package alone. Every entry added here is a reason a build could fail on
# somebody else's machine.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Homepage: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd.git
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/bceverly/tmd
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: tmd
# Not "all": this is a compiled C program, so the package is tied to an
# architecture. "any" rather than a list, because nothing here is
# architecture-specific -- the build works wherever a C11 compiler does.
Architecture: any
Depends:
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
Description: dump the metadata out of a tar archive
 tmd reads a tar archive and reports what is inside it without extracting
 anything. By default it prints one ls(1)-style line per member: the permission
 bits, the owner and group, the size, the modification time the archive
 recorded, and the path.
 .
 BSD archives (ustar and pax, as bsdtar and libarchive write them), GNU tar
 archives including long names and sparse files, and pre-POSIX v7 archives are
 all understood. The format is worked out per member rather than per archive,
 because a real archive mixes them.
 .
 Beyond the listing it can report every header field in long, JSON or CSV form;
 verify header checksums and the end-of-archive marker; and describe the
 archive itself -- which generation of the format it belongs to, which
 extensions it relies on, what wrote it, and what it would take to read it
 somewhere else.
 .
 The archive is only ever opened for reading. Only headers are read, and member
 data is seeked over, so the cost of reading an archive is proportional to how
 many members it has rather than to how large it is.
